.TH simple_shell 1 "Jan 2026" "v1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
simple_shell \- simple UNIX command interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B simple_shell
[\fIcommand\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B simple_shell
program is a simplified UNIX command interpreter written in C. It reads
lines from standard input (interactive mode) or from a pipe/file (non-interactive
mode), parses them, and executes the corresponding commands.

It supports basic command execution, environment handling, and built-in
commands. This implementation was created as part of the Holberton School
curriculum to understand internal shell behavior, process creation,
and environment handling. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

.TP
.B Interactive mode
Displays a prompt "$ " and waits for the user to enter commands.
.TP
.B Non-interactive mode
Reads commands from standard input (e.g., via echo or a file redirection)
and executes them sequentially.

.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS
The following built-ins are supported:

.TP
.B exit
Exits the shell. If an argument is provided (e.g., exit 3), it returns
that as the exit status.
.TP
.B env
Prints the current environment variables.

.SH USAGE
In interactive mode:

.nf
$ ./simple_shell
$ ls -l
$ env
$ exit
.fi

In non-interactive mode:

.nf
echo "ls -la" | ./simple_shell
.fi

.SH EXIT STATUS
Upon successful execution of a command, the exit status of the last process
is returned.

If a command is not found, the shell prints an error message similar
to:

.nf
./hsh: 1: command: not found
.fi

.SH ERRORS
The behavior is undefined if:

.IP \[bu]
Memory allocation fails.
.IP \[bu]
Input exceeds the maximum supported length.

.SH SEE ALSO
sh(1), bash(1)

